<p>Start address of the character screen: one byte per cell holding the screen
code of the character displayed there. The cell at column x, row y is at offset
<strong>y * SCREEN_WIDTH + x</strong>.</p>
<p>The address depends on the machine, and on the VIC 20 also on the memory
expansion the project is built for, so always use the constant rather than a
literal address.</p>
<p>Assign it to a pointer such as the built in <strong>screenmemory</strong> to write
screen codes. Write <strong>#SCREEN_CHAR_LOC</strong> when assigning it to a pointer: a
named address constant assigned bare can be read as an address instead of a
value.</p>

<p>Example</p>
<code>
DefineScreen();

screenmemory := #SCREEN_CHAR_LOC;

// write an A to the top left of the screen
screenmemory[0] := 1;

// and one in the middle of the second row
screenmemory[SCREEN_WIDTH + 10] := 1;
</code>
<p>Note that these are screen codes, not PETSCII or ASCII: A is 1, not 65. See
also SCREEN_COL_LOC for the matching colour cells, and SetColorMemoryAddress to
point colormemory at the same place.</p>
